  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1104 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA calm and warm night is on display across the Mid-South under\nclear skies and mostly calm winds at the surface. Another hot and\ndry day will be present again Tuesday as a 595dam ridge,\ncurrently centered over the eastern Central Plains, continues to\nbroaden east and heights continue to rise. Southerly flow will\nalso usher in slightly increased moisture allowing dewpoints to\ngradually rise across the region. Afternoon highs in the mid to\nlower 100s with dewpoints in the upper 60s to lower 70s will\nresult in heat indices 105+F in the Delta Region, portions of\nnortheast Mississippi, and portions of West Tennessee. Therefore,\na Heat Advisory has been issued and will go into effect from 11AM\nthrough 8PM Tuesday in these areas. A few pop-up showers can't be\nruled out mainly south of the Tennessee and Mississippi state\nline Tuesday afternoon with increased moisture in combination\nwith peak daytime heating. Any precipitation that does fall will\nnot be enough to help ongoing drought conditions and marginal\nfire weather concerns.\n\nWednesday will be largely a rinse and repeat of Tuesday as the\naforementioned ridge continues to dominate the region with hot\nand largely dry conditions. A Heat Advisory will likely be\nwarranted across portions of the Mid-South once again Wednesday,\nhowever, this will greatly depend on dewpoints as ridging begins\nto flatten out ahead of a front. NBM continues to overdo\ndewpoints with the current airmass, therefore, dewpoints have\nbeen hedged down a bit across the Mid-South.\n\nCome Thursday, a pattern change will commence as an upper-level\nlow pressure system starts to dip down over eastern Canada. Weak\ntroughing will begin to move into the Upper Mississippi Valley.\nAn attendant cold front and modest height falls will bring our\nnext shot at appreciable rainfall Thursday into Friday. Rainfall\namounts remain meager as this front will be moving over\npersistent ridging and dry air here in the Mid-South, however, we\nwelcome any rainfall to hopefully help alleviate worsening\ndrought conditions. The latest LREF guidance depicts around a 20-\n35% of >500 J/kg of SBCAPE and 20 kts of 0-6km bulk wind shear\namidst increased forecast PWATs, around 1.8-2.0\",  both Thursday\nand Friday afternoon into evening. We'll be keeping an eye on the\noverall severe potential with this next system amidst the modest\nheight falls and peak daytime heating both days. More refined\ndetails will come into fruition in the coming days. Post-frontal\ntemperatures look to drop about 2-4 degrees with highs in the low\nto mid 90s late weak and into the weekend. We'll still be above\nnormal for this time of the year, but it will feel better than\nthe lower 100s we witnessed this past week.\n\nAEH",
